  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "Let E be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its Borel measurable structure, and let m be any natural number, with nodes Fin(m). Let V:E->R be C^2 and let kappa be a nonnegative real with 1<=kappa. Assume, for every x,v in E,\n  kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nFix eta,c,epsilon in R satisfying 0<eta<=c<1 and epsilon>0. Let nu be a probability measure on E x E and let xstar in E satisfy grad V(xstar)=0. Fix h,M,S>=0. Assume (X,P)->||X-xstar||^2+||P||^2 is integrable under nu with integral <=M. Let t:Fin(m)->R satisfy |t_i|<=1, and omega:Fin(m) x Fin(m)->R satisfy sum_j |omega_ij|<=S, for every i. Let b,d:Fin(m)->R be arbitrary, denoting positionWeight,momentumWeight.\nWrite D=dim_R E, a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), G for the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability law on E, gi=product_{Fin(m)} G, gamma=(G x G) x (gi x gi), and mu=nu x gamma. Under mu the incoming state is independent of all noises, while the two single-vector noises and the two independent finite Gaussian arrays are mutually independent. Set exactly\n  MR=M+(1-exp(-h))D,\n  B=6 MR+3 epsilon^2+3 eta D,\n  C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c),\n  L0=C[1+log(1+sqrt(2 MR)/epsilon)],\n  L1=C[1+log(1+sqrt(4 MR+2 S^2 B)/epsilon)].\nThen there exist global measurable functions p,q:E->E and N:E->N such that for every y,\n  p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y,\n  ||q(y)-p(y)||<=epsilon,\n  run(grad V,eta,epsilon,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1).\nThe exact-real interpreter fails with none at zero fuel. At positive fuel it evaluates g(x), sets next=y-eta g(x), returns (x,1) if ||next-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon, and otherwise recurses from next with one less fuel, adding one to a successful count. Counts include the terminal successful test.\nFor those witnesses and w=((X,P),((zeta0,zeta1),(G0,G1))), define\n  P0(w)=a P+sigma zeta0,\n  Y0_i(w)=X+t_i P0(w),\n  Z0_j(w)=grad V(q(Y0_j(w))+sqrt(eta)G0_j),\n  Y1_i(w)=Y0_i(w)-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j(w),\n  Z1_j(w)=grad V(q(Y1_j(w))+sqrt(eta)G1_j),\n  Phi(w)=(X+h P0(w)-sum_j b_j Z1_j(w),\n          a(P0(w)-sum_j d_j Z1_j(w))+sigma zeta1),\n  T(w)=2m+sum_i[(N(Y0_i(w))+1)+(N(Y1_i(w))+1)] in N.\nThen Phi and T are measurable, and there exists a Markov kernel K from E x E to E x E with, for every incoming state s,\n  K(s)=(z -> Phi(s,z))_* gamma.\nFor every w, one has the exact successful execution identity\n  phaseQuery(grad V,eta,epsilon,h,t,omega,b,d,(y -> N(y)+1),w)\n    =some(Phi(w),T(w)).\nBy its approved definition, phaseQuery computes P0,Y0, sequentially collects m calls to run at the first-stage inputs, and maps each successful pair (r,k) to (g(r+sqrt(eta)G0_i),k+1). It constructs Y1 using the first collected gradient array, then sequentially collects the corresponding m second-stage calls, mapping each success to (g(r+sqrt(eta)G1_i),k+1). It returns the displayed final update and sum of collected counts; a failed call propagates none. collectQueries returns empty array/count zero at m=0, otherwise collects the first node and recursive tail and adds counts. Thus the asserted success counts all interpreter tests plus one extra shifted-gradient evaluation per node in each stage, with total overhead exactly 2m. This definition is explicitly linked in the approved context, without any additional theorem premise.\nFinally w->(T(w):R) is Bochner integrable under mu and\n  integral (T(w):R) dmu(w) <= (m:R)(2+L0+L1).\nAll sums range over all m actual nodes. The theorem establishes global successful exact-real execution, witness/error/measurability conditions, a probability transition kernel and this total expected abstract gradient-evaluation bound. It asserts no sampler invariant law, convergence, bias, final-state moment bound, finite-precision result, source-specific quadrature contract or wall-clock complexity.",
